Found a real bug: the level tiles are opaque materials so they render in the opaque pass, but the parallax is transparent and renders *after* — so the backdrop paints over the playfield.
[0m
[0m$ [0mcd "/home/nico/Documents/U/Co/U/4s/courses/interfaces/tareas/proyecto final/juego" && python3 - <<'PY'
p='js/game/level.js'; s=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()
s=s.replace("""    const mat = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color, transparent: opacity < 1, opacity });""",
"""    // transparent:true on purpose, even at full opacity. Three renders the
    // opaque pass before the transparent pass, and the parallax layers are
    // transparent -- so opaque tiles drew FIRST and the backdrop painted over
    // the playfield. In the transparent pass these sort by z and land between
    // the far backdrop (z=-40) and the foreground band (z=+6), which is the
    // order they need to be in.
    const mat = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color, transparent: true, opacity });""")
s=s.replace("    const mat = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color });",
            "    const mat = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({ color, transparent: true });")
open(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)
print('tiles moved to the transparent pass')
