Stage 1b: rebuild the far backdrop

The backdrops were a cover-fit of the whole portrait painting, tiled to fill.
That showed a random vertical slice and produced huge blurry blocks with
visible seams. L3 also read dark blue, because 3.jpg is a demon portrait with
text in it rather than a burnt-orange sky.

Now each far backdrop is drawn once into a 600x338 canvas -- view width plus
the 120px of slack the pan needs -- and never tiled. A purpose-made
assets/bg/lN.png listed in the manifest is used whole when present; otherwise
a fixed 16:9 crop of the painting (L1 the arch and statues, L2 the gothic arch)
and a procedural burnt-orange gradient over bare trees for L3. Brightness is
applied on the material, not baked into the canvas, and mid trunks are pinned
to pure black at 35% so they cannot drift.

Two root causes, not just tuning:

- The far quad was centred on the camera rather than the view, so it covered
  only the left half of the screen and the right half was raw sky. That is what
  the brief saw as blurred blocks, and it dragged L2's mean hue to 237deg.
  Centred on the view the crop spans the viewport exactly once at every pan
  value.
- The scene pass stored UnsignedByte, but linear light clusters near zero, so
  8 bits per channel destroyed the dark half of every colour: an sRGB (30,14,5)
  landed on (3,1,0). Burnt orange measured as yellow-green. The target is now
  HalfFloat, which is a one-line change outside the backdrop but is what makes
  the hues measurable at all.

tools/stage1b.mjs captures l1/l2/l3 and checks them: 14/14 pass, backdrop mean
hues 124 / 260 / 24deg against the 80-160 / 240-300 / 10-40 windows.
