Stage 2: prologue, elder deal, theft cutscene with real art

The opening of the story, end to end: menu -> prologue -> L1 -> the elder's
deal -> the theft -> back to play with the elder gone.

Cutscene panels are real art now. `line.art` of '01'..'06' resolves to
assets/cutscenes/NN.png and is drawn with object-fit contain in the strip
between the letterbox bars, with the same image again behind it as a blurred
cover so the square panels 03 and 04 have no black side bars. All six are
preloaded when a new game starts so no panel pops in mid-sentence. Each panel
pushes in from scale(1.00) to scale(1.06) over the time it is up, alternating
the transform origin left/right, and two layers crossfade over 400ms. No
pixelation filter on these -- they are painted stills, not sprites.

The prologue replaces `intro` as the new-game cutscene: narration only, no
speaker name, over stills 01 and 02. The theft is a new script over 05 and 06
with per-line speakers. Both languages go through strings.js.

The elder moved to x=30 tiles on the floor, facing left (goodwitch's sheet is
drawn facing right, so that is a flip). His spot is a contract, not a comment:
level.js asserts flat ground for 8 tiles each way, nothing within 6 tiles
overhead, and no enemy spawn or patrol inside 20 tiles, and throws if a later
layout change breaks it. The assertion earned its keep immediately -- it caught
a bug in the check itself, where I had treated a patrol range as a radius
around the spawn instead of the stretch it can actually wander.

The deal is a new S.STORY state, so the level keeps rendering and only the two
characters simulate under it. Kael stops, turns to the elder, and the camera
eases onto both of them before the first line is spoken. Letterbox bars in,
portraits on each side, speaker name above the line, first press finishes the
line and second advances -- the same rule as a cutscene. Panels 03 and 04 fade
in over the level on their own lines and back out on the first unmarked one.
Portraits are the idle frame cropped to the head and shoulders and blown up 3x
with smoothing off; the crop finds the figure by alpha first, because the
sheets pad their cells heavily (the goodwitch frame is 40x49 but the figure
fills 26x44 of it) and a blind corner crop frames padding.

The theft cutscene then plays, the elder is removed from the scene, and a puff
of toxic green is left drifting where he stood -- given a longer life than a
combat burst on purpose, so it is still there when control comes back. Esc
skips a whole cutscene rather than one line per press.

The dead `theft` script and its cs2* strings are gone. `intro` is kept: BOSS_INTRO
still plays it as the level 2 boss cutscene, so the brief's "if nothing else
uses them" does not apply. Its lines now contradict the deal and are listed in
the report rather than rewritten.

tools/story.mjs is the acceptance run and passes 10/10, exit 0, zero console
errors, with all seven screenshots read and checked by eye. tools/reach.mjs
still passes (19/20/24 surfaces hold the player, all reachable, jump apex
132/94/94 against a floor+55 bar) after being taught to mute the deal: the
elder now stands where the test teleports, and the proximity trigger was
freezing the body mid-measurement. Its jump assertion was also frame-rate
flaky, because the game clamps dt and a 350ms key hold is worth less
simulated time under a software rasteriser, so the variable-jump cut cut into
the apex; each press is now held for its full slot. Stable over four runs.
